<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agadir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agadir is a city on the Atlantic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. The Portuguese built a fortress there in 1505, known as Santa Cruz do Cabo de Gué, which the Saadi ruler Muhammad al-Shaykh captured in 1541, a victory that led Portugal to abandon several other Moroccan ports. Destroyed by an earthquake in 1960, the city was rebuilt and is now a major port and resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agadir.jpg" alt="Agadir" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdeaitali · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beach_of_Agadir_from_Kasbah_of_Agadir_Oufella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
